{"name":"White Jasmine","hex":"#f7f4df","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"jasmine","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several plants, of the genus Jasminum, mostly native to Asia, having fragrant white or yellow flowers.","The perfume obtained from these plants."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jasmine"}],"entries":[{"title":"White jasmine","blocks":[{"text":"White jasmine is a common name for several plants and may refer to:"},{"items":["Jasminum officinale, commonly cultivated as an ornamental plant","Jasminum polyanthum, commonly cultivated as a houseplant"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_jasmine"},{"title":"Jasminum officinale","blocks":[{"text":"Jasminum officinale, known as the common jasmine or simply jasmine, is a species of flowering plant in the olive family Oleaceae. It is native to the Caucasus and parts of Asia, also widely naturalized."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Jasminum_officinale_-_Bot._Mag._31%2C_1787.jpg/330px-Jasminum_officinale_-_Bot._Mag._31%2C_1787.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasminum_officinale"},{"title":"Jasmine","blocks":[{"text":"Jasmine is a genus of shrubs and vines in the olive family of Oleaceae. It contains around 200 species native to tropical and warm temperate regions of Asia, Africa, and Oceania. Jasmines are widely cultivated for the characteristic fragrance of their flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Common_Jasmine.jpg/330px-Common_Jasmine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jasmine"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Jasminum_officinale.JPG/500px-Jasminum_officinale.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Jasminum_officinale.JPG/1280px-Jasminum_officinale.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Jasminum_officinale.JPG/500px-Jasminum_officinale.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJasminum_officinale.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/JasmineAbsolute.png/500px-JasmineAbsolute.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/JasmineAbsolute.png/500px-JasmineAbsolute.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Essential oil",